-## Subtopics
-
-### Cone Tracing AO
-An extension by Wright [S1, unofficial] that replaces the simple normal sampling with cone tracing. Cones originate at the surface and are tested for intersection against the SDF. This provides more accurate occlusion for complex geometry but is more expensive.
-
-### Linear vs. Quadratic Sampling
-Linear sampling [S3, S4, unofficial] is simpler but may miss occluders in sparse regions. Quadratic sampling [S2, unofficial] increases sample density further from the surface, better capturing distant occluders for ambient light.
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Self-Intersection (Acne):** Floating-point precision errors can cause the sample point to be slightly inside the geometry, resulting in negative distances and incorrect occlusion. A small bias offset along the normal is required [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** The accuracy of the AO depends on the resolution of the SDF. Low-resolution SDFs may produce blocky or inaccurate occlusion.
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** At the boundaries of the SDF grid, queries may return undefined or large values. Proper handling of grid boundaries is necessary [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Parameter Tuning:** The scale, decay, and number of samples significantly affect the visual result. Too few samples may result in noisy or inaccurate AO, while too many may be computationally expensive [S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per surface point, where $N$ is the number of samples. Independent of scene complexity, depending only on SDF query cost.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query if SDF is implicit, or $O(V)$ for grid-based SDF storage, where $V$ is the volume of the grid.
-- **Performance:** Very fast compared to ray tracing or SSAA with multiple samples. Suitable for real-time applications [S1, unofficial][S5].
-- **Strengths:**
-  - Geometrically accurate compared to SSAO [S5].
-  - No dependence on screen space or depth buffers [S5].
-  - Works well for volumetric and implicit surfaces [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:**
-  - Approximate; may not capture complex indirect lighting bounces [S1, unofficial].
-  - Directional bias (normal-only sampling) may look incorrect on flat surfaces or in tight corners [S5].
-  - Requires an SDF representation of the scene [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of samples per pixel. Each sample requires an SDF query, which is $O(1)$ for a grid-based SDF or $O(\log M)$ for a hierarchical SDF (e.g., Voxel Grid) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the SDF storage. A dense 3D grid requires $O(V)$ memory where $V$ is the volume of the grid. Sparse representations (e.g., Octrees, VDB) can reduce this significantly [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Generally faster than ray-traced AO but slower than SSAO due to the need for SDF queries. Performance scales with the number of samples and SDF resolution [S1, unofficial][S5].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides accurate occlusion in global context, handles self-occlusion well, and works well with dynamic geometry if the SDF is updated [S5].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires an SDF representation of the scene, which can be memory-intensive for large scenes. SDF updates can be costly [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The technique was introduced by Evans [S1, unofficial] as a method to embed objects into a 3D grid of signed distance fields, where occlusion is calculated via a heuristic combining values sampled at increasing distances along the surface normal. It was later expanded by Wright [S1, unofficial] who substituted the ad hoc heuristic with cone tracing. The method is widely used in real-time ray marching and voxel-based rendering engines due to its efficiency and visual quality [S1, unofficial][S5].
+The technique was introduced by Evans, who embedded objects into a 3D grid of signed distance fields and calculated occlusion via a heuristic combining values sampled at increasing distances along the surface normal [S1, unofficial]. Wright expanded on this approach by substituting the ad hoc heuristic with cone tracing, approximated by sphere tracing [S1, unofficial]. The method is widely used in real-time rendering, particularly in voxel-based global illumination systems, to provide ambient occlusion that is consistent with the global geometry [S1, unofficial][S5].
 
